INSERT INTO phpbb_privmsgs_text VALUES (19875, '0c96cd7031', 'Thanks for the props on the Camaro, that means more coming from you than anyone.\r\n\r\nThe "equivalent to a 38mm lens on a 35mm camera" thing is a little confusing... those little lenses in the digital world are their own beast, so they compare to the 35mm equivalent to confuse matters further. In 35mm land, a 50mm lens is considered "normal", meaning that it sees about the same as the human eye. Wider lenses, like a 28mm, are good for shooting interior because they see a wider periphery, but they distort at the edges. They also over exaggerate depth... things that are close look huge and things that aren\'t that far away look tiny. With long lenses, things that are near and far from the camera look more equal in scale, so things tend to look closer in depth than they actually are. \r\n\r\nAnyway, 38mm is aproaching normal but is on the wide side... which is good for general use. It should actually be great for little cars. The main thing is going to be using that macro mode and figuring out the close-focus range. Do some tests and figure out how close something can be to the camera before the camera can\'t focus on it. My guess is it\'s probably going to be somewhere around 7-10 inches from the lens. I just looked at a sample macro shot from a D-390 [url=http://www.imaging-resource.com/PRODS/D390/FULLRES/D39MAC.HTM]here[/url] so it\'s gonna work... those coins are pretty sharp. You\'ll probably have to shoot with the car a little bit small in the frame, then crop in on it in Photoshop or another editing tool.\r\n\r\nThe lamp I use is 50 watts, and I use a tripod because that\'s still pretty low light to hold the camera steady enough by hand. A tripod or a way to secure the camera is good. Daylight might be your best option.. the camera is gonna use a way faster shutter speed with that much more light. So, outside or a lamp and a tripod, but you don\'t wanna use a flash for things that close up.\r\n\r\nHere, I just read something else that might help out... "The D-390 captured a fairly large macro area, measuring 7.18 x 5.38 inches"\r\nSo, draw a box 7.18 x 5.38 inches and, with the digital zoom off and the camera pointed straight at the page (important), position the camera so that you can see th whole box on the LCD... the distance from the paper to the lens is where you wanna be. Maybe even an inch more just to be sure... any closer is too close for the camera to focus. Measure that distance and always have your car at least that far away from the camera.. if it\'s too small in the frame, crop it later.\r\n\r\nI hope this helps, and feel free to ask me more questions... I\'m sure some of that was confusing.\r\n\r\nOh... also, I\'ll need your new address. I\'m slackin\' on the next batch of discs for you, so I\'ll be shippin\' them to your new place. I\'m probably a week out, to be realistic.\r\n\r\nWell, I hope your luck takes a turn for the better real soon... and remember ya got support in da hood when shit gets bad... later, man.\r\n\r\nMat');
